WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:02.265
Board exam coming up?

2
00:00:02.225 --> 00:00:03.440
Take a breath.

3
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:06.927
You've already done the hard
part: you learned the material.

4
00:00:06.937 --> 00:00:11.777
Now passing is about showing what you
know, in the exact format your board uses.

5
00:00:11.787 --> 00:00:14.340
Here's how to do that, in three minutes.

6
00:00:15.502 --> 00:00:19.167
AnyExamEasy is one study
system for six boards.

7
00:00:19.127 --> 00:00:24.249
You practice in the style of your real
exam, learn why every answer is right or

8
00:00:24.259 --> 00:00:29.317
wrong, and track your progress until test
day feels familiar, not frightening.

9
00:00:30.496 --> 00:00:37.566
NCLEX-RN is adaptive: 85 to 150 items in
up to five hours, and it stops once a pass

10
00:00:37.576 --> 00:00:39.698
or fail decision is made.

11
00:00:39.658 --> 00:00:43.948
Even the shortest exam includes three
clinical judgment case studies.

12
00:00:43.958 --> 00:00:49.443
So practice the Next Generation item types,
and on select-all-that-apply, judge each

13
00:00:49.453 --> 00:00:52.648
option on its own, because
partial credit counts.

14
00:00:53.818 --> 00:01:00.958
NAPLEX is 225 questions in six hours, on a
fixed form, and you can't skip or go back.

15
00:01:00.918 --> 00:01:03.720
Most questions are built
on a patient profile.

16
00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:08.220
Read the whole profile first, then
commit to your answer and keep moving.

17
00:01:09.388 --> 00:01:15.443
For USMLE, Step 1 is now fourteen thirty-minute
blocks of up to twenty questions, in

18
00:01:15.453 --> 00:01:18.765
one eight-hour day, and
it's scored pass/fail.

19
00:01:18.725 --> 00:01:22.553
Step 2 CK now uses sixteen
thirty-minute blocks.

20
00:01:22.563 --> 00:01:28.565
So train in timed thirty-minute blocks, and review
every miss by the mechanism, not just the fact.

21
00:01:29.734 --> 00:01:36.599
PANCE gives you 300 multiple-choice questions in
five blocks of sixty, with an hour for each block.

22
00:01:36.559 --> 00:01:38.811
Once you leave a block, it's closed.

23
00:01:38.821 --> 00:01:44.324
Pace yourself at about a minute a question, and
plan your forty-five minutes of break time.

24
00:01:45.496 --> 00:01:50.536
The AANP FNP exam is 150
questions in three hours.

25
00:01:50.496 --> 00:01:56.610
135 are scored and 15 are unscored pretest
items you can't spot, so treat every

26
00:01:56.620 --> 00:01:58.548
question as if it counts.

27
00:01:58.558 --> 00:02:04.048
Assessment is the biggest domain, and
patients run from newborns to older adults.

28
00:02:04.058 --> 00:02:06.336
Study across the whole lifespan.

29
00:02:07.498 --> 00:02:15.500
NPTE-PT has 225 questions across five sections
of 45, with five hours of testing time.

30
00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:19.588
There's no penalty for a wrong
answer, so answer every question.

31
00:02:19.598 --> 00:02:24.638
Scenario-based questions come at the end
of each section, so save time for them.

32
00:02:25.804 --> 00:02:31.071
On AnyExamEasy, every question is explained,
with a reason for each option, so you

33
00:02:31.081 --> 00:02:34.481
learn from the wrong answers,
not just the right one.

34
00:02:34.441 --> 00:02:39.565
You'll practice NGN and case-style questions,
take full practice exams, and see a

35
00:02:39.575 --> 00:02:42.544
readiness estimate built
from your own answers.

36
00:02:42.554 --> 00:02:50.269
Content review is led by Ryan Chishimba, PharmD,
with NCLEX review led by Ileen Chishimba, RN.

37
00:02:51.430 --> 00:02:52.745
You've got this.

38
00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:54.782
Start your five-day free trial today.

39
00:02:54.792 --> 00:03:00.532
Pick your exam, answer your first question,
and walk into your board exam calm and ready.
